  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 355 AM EDT Saturday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Unsettled and humid through Sunday as a frontal boundary\nlifts north later today, followed by a cold frontal passage late\nSunday. Numerous showers, thunderstorms, and localized heavy \nrain today-tonight, with scattered showers/storms Sunday.\n\nThe latest WX analysis indicates a frontal boundary nearly\nstationary across northern NC. A strong upper level trough is \nnoted on WV satellite imagery across the lower MS Valley, and \nthis trough will slowly translate eastward towards the local \narea through tonight. SW flow aloft strengthens today, and \nshould help lift the frontal boundary back to north as a warm \nfront this aftn into this evening. A few spotty showers prevail \nin the moist humid airmass early this morning, but most areas \nwill remain dry through midday. The recent CAMs are somewhat \nsplit on shower/storm development later today, and taking a \nblend generally develops low chc PoPs by late morning/early \naftn, with PoPs increasing to likely for most of the area after\n~20-21Z (though with lower PoPs for the eastern shore). \nWidespread precipitation chances peak after this and persist \ninto the evening, as the stalled boundary lifts back north as a \nwarm front, along with a more pronounced mid- level shortwave \ndisturbances push across the area. The latest models show the \nbest height falls aloft late-gnerally after midnight through \nsunrise Sunday, so even after the best instability wanes, this \nadditional cold air pool aloft should be sufficient to keep \nhigh chc to likely PoPs continuing overnight. Forecast soundings\ncontinue to show high precipitable water values, modest \ninstability (skinny CAPE profile), and weak steering flow, which\nshould yield efficient warm rainfall processes and additional \nlocalized hydro concerns (supported by an area-wide WPC ERO \nMarginal Risk and a localized Slight for portions of the I-95 \ncorridor, including metro RIC. Despite the increasing confidence\nthat localized flooding is possible somewhere in our forecast \narea, there still remains uncertainty in the exact locations, so\nhave decided to not go with a Flood Watch for later today into \ntonight. The 00Z/12 HREF, which often provides a good signal for\nareas where the highest rainfall amounts, is not honing in on \nany specific area at this time with the 3\"/3hr PMM peaked at \nonly 10%. Considerable cloud cover will keep a lid on today's \nhighs- the low 80s NW to the upper 80s SE. The boundary washes \nout Sunday, leading to noticeably lower convective coverage for \nthe NW 1/2 of the area, but PoPs remain high chc to likely for \nthe SE and some late day storms are still possible in the NW as \nwell. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Mainly dry with seasonable temperatures and\nsignificantly lower humidity Monday-Wednesday. Temperatures \nwarm back above normal late in the week an upper ridge builds \nback into the region.\n\nThe upper level flow becomes NW on Monday as a strong upper level \ntrough dives SE from the Great Lakes and pushes off the NE and mid-\nAtlantic coast Monday night, with an upper level ridge slowly \nexpanding NE from the lower MS Valley. This looks strong enough to \ndrive the sfc cold front well south of the CWA Monday, and bring\nsignificantly lower dew pts more typical of September. The models\nhave trended a little farther south and slower with the shortwave\non Monday, so do expect to see a 3-6 hr window where clouds \nincrease and even a few isolated showers develop on Monday \nbefore the much drier air surges in from the NNW during the \nafternoon (with PoPs lingering through the aftn across the \nsouthern areas). As high pressure builds in behind the front \nMonday night, a pleasant night is expected Monday night/Tue AM \nwith lows mostly in the upper 50s to lower 60s (low-mid 50s are\nlikely in many locations to the NW of Richmond). The models have\ntrended a bit slower with the return flow of moisture so that\nnow dew pts look to remain comfortable through at least\nWednesday as strong sfc high pressure (nearly 1030 mb), sets up\nfrom off the New England coast SW into the Carolina coastal\nplain through midweek. Highs will stay mostly in the low-mid 80s\nthrough Wed with lows in the 50s to lower 60s (locally warmer at\nthe immediate coast). The upper level ridge is forecast to\nexpand E-NE to the TN Valley and central Appalachians Thu-Fri. \nTemperatures climb back to above normal for mid- September, \nwith highs generally in the mid-upper 80s. Low end diurnal rain\nchances return Thu-Fri (but have trended drier/lower form the\nprevious forecast cycle).",
